# Mutation cases for §scxml-3.13 read against the other half of the case the
# specification explicitly allows — a macrostep whose `<raise>` chain never
# ends.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/internal_chain_is_bounded.cases`.
#
# `eventless_macrostep_is_bounded` owns the chain built from transitions that
# need no event; this one owns the chain built from internal events. Both are
# the same clause: a macrostep ends where nothing is enabled by NULL AND the
# internal queue is empty, and Appendix D says that end need not exist. Until
# 2026-08-20 the ceiling covered only the eventless branch, so the `<raise>`
# document did not return on any of the seven engines.
#
# Two traps these cases exist for. The first is a ceiling that swallows the
# events it refuses: the fixture's `resume` chain outlives one macrostep and
# must finish in the next, which only holds if the refusal left the queue
# alone. The second is a per-branch budget: the `alternate` chain takes every
# other microstep on each branch, so two counters of a thousand each never fire
# and `alts == 500` is what says the budget is one.
#
# The C++ halves and the C11 template live in
# `internal_chain_is_bounded_ctest.cases`.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-rust-tests --test internal_chain_is_bounded
mutation_targets backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs
mutation_oracles backends/rust/tests/tests/internal_chain_is_bounded.rs

# ── The refusal itself ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The drain keeps taking internal events. This is the shipped behaviour the
# axis replaced — with no budget on this branch the call does not come back,
# and the harness's artefact timeout is what turns "the round hung" into a
# verdict.

mutation_case "the internal drain has no ceiling at all" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """            if self.macrostep_microsteps_taken == MAX_MACROSTEP_MICROSTEPS {
                // Work is still queued one microstep past the budget, so this""",
     """            if false {
                // Work is still queued one microstep past the budget, so this""")
PY

# ── The refusal leaves the queue alone ─────────────────────────────
#
# Popping before asking consumes the event the drain then declines to run. The
# chain is still bounded and every count still moves, so only
# `a_refused_chain_is_left_queued_for_the_next_macrostep` can see it: the
# `resume` chain stops a link short of its end and never finishes, because the
# link that would have continued it was thrown away.

mutation_case "the drain swallows the event it refused" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """                self.record_truncated_macrostep(self.current_state);
                #[cfg(not(feature = "no_macrostep_diagnostics"))]""",
     """                let _swallowed = self.internal_queue.pop();
                self.record_truncated_macrostep(self.current_state);
                #[cfg(not(feature = "no_macrostep_diagnostics"))]""")
PY

# ── One budget, not one per branch ─────────────────────────────────
#
# The internal branch keeps a counter of its own, which is what the Kotlin
# engine shipped. Nothing hangs and the `spin` outcome still reports exactly
# one refusal — the chain that gets through is the one that alternates, because
# each branch keeps finding its own counter unspent. `alts` is the only reading
# that separates them: five hundred when the budget is shared, a thousand when
# it is not.

mutation_case "the internal branch budgets itself" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """    pub(crate) fn process_internal_queue(&mut self) {
        if self.macrostep_truncated {""",
     """    pub(crate) fn process_internal_queue(&mut self) {
        // MUTANT: a budget of this drain's own, reset on every entry.
        self.macrostep_microsteps_taken = 0;
        if self.macrostep_truncated {""")
PY

# ── The microstep that was answered is the one that is counted ─────
#
# Counting a dispatch that selected nothing spends the budget on events the
# macrostep did not act on. The `bounded` chain ends exactly at the ceiling, so
# any over-count reports a document that settles on its own as a runaway.

mutation_case "an unanswered internal event still spends the budget" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """            if outcome != EventOutcome::Discarded {""",
     """            if true {""")
PY

# ── The report ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The chain is still cut, so nothing hangs and every configuration assertion
# still passes — the machine simply stops saying that the configuration it
# hands back is not a stable one. This is the shape the Kotlin engine shipped
# on this branch: bounded and silent, which is the same signal as unbounded to
# the host reading it.

mutation_case "the internal refusal is silent" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """            self.truncated_macrosteps = self.truncated_macrosteps.saturating_add(1);
            self.last_truncated_macrostep_state = Some(state);""",
     """            self.last_truncated_macrostep_state = Some(state);""")
PY

# ── Naming where it stopped ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The count alone says a document somewhere cannot settle. Naming a state on
# the chain is what an author acts on, and the fixture's chains never leave one
# state so the name is unambiguous in all seven channels.

# ⚠ The mutant also consumes `state`. Dropping the only use of a parameter is
# an `unused_variables` warning, this crate denies warnings, and a mutant that
# does not compile reports INCONCLUSIVE — a case that says nothing where it
# used to say CAUGHT. Measured 2026-08-21, when the diagnostics feature gate
# moved this assignment inside a `#[cfg]` block.
mutation_case "the internal refusal counts but never names the state" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """            self.last_truncated_macrostep_state = Some(state);""",
     """            self.last_truncated_macrostep_state = None;
            let _ = state;""")
PY

# ── The macrostep boundary ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A budget that is never given back makes the ceiling a property of the
# machine's whole life: the events the first macrostep refused stay queued
# forever, because every later macrostep finds the budget already spent.
# `a_refused_chain_is_left_queued_for_the_next_macrostep` reads the chain's
# end for it — the second macrostep has to be able to finish what the first
# would not.

mutation_case "the spent budget outlives the macrostep that spent it" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """        self.macrostep_truncated = false;
        self.macrostep_microsteps_taken = 0;
        {
            // §scxml-6.5: Execute finalize before parent's own transition matching""",
     """        {""")
PY
